213	/// Deterministic remapping of device numbers (`st_dev`).
214	///
215	/// The kernel assigns anonymous block-device numbers to filesystems without a
216	/// backing block device (procfs, sysfs, tmpfs, devpts) from a global,
217	/// host-wide counter (`get_anon_bdev`). The raw `st_dev` a guest observes for
218	/// such a filesystem therefore drifts between otherwise-identical runs — and
219	/// even between the two runs of `--verify`, because the first run's mounts are
220	/// still live when the second run mounts fresh copies, so the second run's
221	/// procfs gets a different anonymous device number. That leaked host state into
222	/// a guest-visible `stat`/`statx` field.
223	///
224	/// We replace each distinct raw device number with a strictly-increasing
225	/// synthetic id assigned in first-observation order. Both `stat`/`statx` and a
226	/// virtualized mountinfo snapshot use this pool. A mountinfo read intentionally
227	/// pre-populates it in that snapshot's row order, and later metadata syscalls
228	/// reuse those assignments.
229	///
230	/// This guarantees identity consistency within one Detcore run. It is not an
231	/// unconditional cross-machine guarantee: hosts with different filesystem
232	/// layouts can expose different device equivalence classes or first-observation
233	/// order. The remapping still preserves distinctness and equality within the
234	/// run, so `find -xdev`, `du -x`, and `(st_dev, st_ino)` checks behave
235	/// consistently with the mountinfo device column.
236	#[derive(Debug)]
237	struct DevicePool {
238	    devices: HashMap<u64, u64>,
239	    next_device: u64,
240	}
